Idea: {"Name": "bias_stiffness_hysteron_duality", "Title": "Bias\u2013Stiffness 
Duality in Mechanical Hysteron Networks: From Threshold Collapse to Programmable
Eight-Cycles", "Short Hypothesis": "A coupling spring acts on a bistable rotor 
through two inseparable geometric channels: a state-dependent bias that shifts 
the switching center, c_i = (gamma_up + gamma_down)/2, and a state-dependent 
curvature that shrinks the hysteresis half-width, h_i = (gamma_up - 
gamma_down)/2. For a spring attached at lever arm L, the bias should scale 
linearly with L while the curvature scales as L^2, so h_i(L) = h_i(0) - K_i L^2 
and c_i(s_j,L) = c_i(0) + J_i(L) s_j. This single law should explain why pair_A 
thresholds collapse inward with mounting distance, why pair_B crossed springs 
produce much larger common-mode center shifts, and why network_C executes one 
ordered 8-state limit cycle rather than an arbitrary transition graph. The best 
test is pair_A, where L varies with all else fixed, combined with the topology 
change in pair_B and the ordered events in network_C; threshold shifts alone can
always be absorbed into an arbitrary Preisach field, but the predicted width 
contraction and its L^2 scaling are not.", "Related Work": "Interacting-hysteron
work has shown that serial mechanical hysterons can realize geometrically 
controlled antiferromagnetic-like interactions and finite-state-machine behavior
(PNAS 2024), and that connected balloons can be described by Preisach hysterons 
whose transition graphs change under volume control (PRE 2024). Mechanical 
metamaterials have also been designed to count cycles and process sequences (PRL
2023). These studies treat interactions mainly as threshold shifts or targeted 
transition graphs. The proposed bias\u2013stiffness duality is distinct: it 
derives both the center shift and the hysteresis-width contraction from the same
spring-energy expansion, predicts a specific quadratic lever-arm law, and uses 
that law to predict the topology and amplitude phase diagram of a four-rotor 
mechanical automaton. It is not a trivial extension of Preisach modeling because
it tests a geometric relation between two usually separate phenomenological 
parameters.", "Abstract": "Mechanical hysterons are bistable units whose 
switching thresholds can be shifted by coupling. Existing models usually 
represent coupling as a state-dependent bias, leaving the hysteresis width 
unchanged. We propose that a coupling spring necessarily produces two channels: 
a bias torque, linear in the spring's lever arm L, and a curvature term, 
quadratic in L, that lowers the barrier and contracts the hysteresis width. 
Expanding the spring energy for rotor angle theta_i gives U_c = const + 
tau_ij(L) s_j theta_i + (1/2) kappa_ij(L) theta_i^2 + ..., so the measured 
thresholds obey c_i(s_j,L) = c_i0 + J_i(L) s_j and h_i(L) = h_i0 - K_i(L), with 
K_i proportional to L^2 for a fixed pretension. We test this on supplied 
measurements: pair_A varies coupling attachment distance L from 0 to 4.731 cm; 
pair_B changes from parallel to crossed coupling; network_C supplies 16 ordered 
state events forming an eight-state cycle. The data already show the predicted 
pattern: for pair_A rotor 1, h falls from 7.2 to 4.6 cm while the center shift 
grows approximately linearly; rotor 2, with a -1.9 cm mounting offset, shows a 
stronger, apparently quadratic center shift. Crossed pair_B springs produce much
larger common-mode shifts. We then build a four-hysteron race model and predict 
that reducing drive amplitude should truncate the 8-cycle into smaller 
subcycles, while reversing one coupling sign should reverse or split the cycle. 
The result would turn mechanical hysteresis from a threshold lookup table into a
geometrically constrained finite-state machine.", "Experiments": "1. Calibration
reanalysis: fit each spring's loading/unloading force-extension curve with F = 
k(x-x0) plus a small hysteretic offset. Quantify stiffness and friction; use 
only as priors because calibration samples are not exact installed springs. 2. 
Pair_A threshold decomposition: for every (rotor_i, direction, other_state, 
mount_l_cm), compute c=(gamma_up+gamma_down)/2 and h=(gamma_up-gamma_down)/2. 
Fit c_i(s_j,L)=c_i0+J_i(L)s_j and h_i(L)=h_i0-K_i L^2. Report R^2, 
leave-one-L-out prediction error, and whether rotor 1 is linear in L while rotor
2 is quadratic, as expected from its -1.9 cm offset. 3. Pair_B topology test: 
fit the same decomposition to the eight crossed-spring rows. Test the ordinal 
prediction that crossed coupling gives larger |J| and opposite sign relative to 
pair_A at comparable L; because only one L is available, treat this as a 
sign/magnitude test, not a precise curve fit. 4. Network_C race model: implement
four hysterons with thresholds gamma_i^\u00b1(s)=c_i+sum_j J_ij s_j \u00b1 . 
Under monotone drive segments, flip the rotor whose threshold is crossed first. 
Because network_C connectivity and stop angles are unknown, infer only 
qualitative constraints from the observed 16-event sequence and event drive 
values. Use cycle edit distance and whether the observed 8-cycle is a stable 
periodic orbit. 5. Proposed inexpensive perturbation: on network_C vary one 
coupling attachment distance or swap one parallel/crossed pair, and vary drive 
amplitude. Predict a period-8 to period-4/2/1 phase diagram, with the highest 
positive events (5.8-5.9 cm) disappearing first as amplitude is reduced, and a 
cycle reversal or split when one coupling sign is reversed. Metrics: threshold 
prediction error in cm, event-order accuracy, cycle period, cycle edit distance,
and phase-boundary location.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Network_C's full
connectivity, stop angles, attachment coordinates, and drive reversal positions 
are unknown, so its quantitative prediction is weak. The supplied rows are 
figure-source measurements, not independent repetitions; there are no error bars
for thresholds or events, and no timestamps. Calibration samples are not exact 
identities of installed springs. Pair_B has only one mounting distance, so 
topology and lever-arm effects are confounded. The small-angle, pairwise, 
quasistatic spring model may fail near stops or when springs go slack; rotor 2's
nonlinear center shift may indicate such a regime. The observed 8-cycle could be
protocol-dependent, and two repeated cycles are not enough to establish 
stability. The proposal is explanatory and conditional: failure of the L^2 width
law or of the predicted amplitude truncation would falsify the dual-channel 
mechanism."}
